# 3. Now understand the difference between your FLEX code and this YACC code

This is the **most important concept**.

Your original Flex program does this:

```text
Input
  ↓
LEX
  ↓
Identify individual tokens
```

For example:

```c
switch(x)
```

Flex sees:

```text
SWITCH
LPAREN
ID
RPAREN
```

But Flex doesn't really ask:

> "Is this a valid switch statement?"

YACC does that.

```text
Input
  ↓
LEX
  ↓
Tokens
  ↓
YACC
  ↓
Grammar checking
  ↓
Valid / Invalid
```

---

# 4. What is a grammar?

YACC works using **grammar rules**.

For example:

```c
switch_statement:
        SWITCH LPAREN expression RPAREN
        LBRACE
        case_list
        RBRACE
        ;
```

This says:

> A valid `switch_statement` must have `switch`, followed by `(`, an expression, `)`, `{`, a list of cases, and finally `}`.

In simplified form:

```text
switch_statement
       ↓
switch ( expression ) { case_list }
```

---

# 5. Understanding the YACC syntax

A YACC rule looks like:

```text
non_terminal:
        production
        ;
```

For example:

```c
case_block:
        CASE constant COLON statement_list
        ;
```

This means:

```text
case_block
    ↓
CASE + constant + : + statement_list
```

So:

```c
case 10:
    printf("Ten");
```

can match this grammar.

---

# 6. Why do we use `%token`?

At the beginning you have:

```c
%token SWITCH
%token CASE DEFAULT
```

These are tokens that come from Lex.

For example, Lex has:

```c
"switch" {
    return SWITCH;
}
```

So when Lex reads:

```text
switch
```

it returns:

```text
SWITCH
```

to YACC.

Similarly:

```c
"case" {
    return CASE;
}
```

returns:

```text
CASE
```

---

# 7. Communication between Lex and YACC

This is the key architecture:

```text
                 INPUT
                   │
                   ▼
              FLEX / LEX
                   │
                   │ Tokens
                   ▼
              YACC / BISON
                   │
                   ▼
              Grammar Rules
                   │
             ┌─────┴─────┐
             ▼           ▼
          Valid        Invalid
```

For example:

```c
switch(x) {
    case 1:
        break;
}
```

Flex produces:

```text
SWITCH
LPAREN
ID
RPAREN
LBRACE
CASE
INT_CONST
COLON
BREAK
SEMICOLON
RBRACE
```

YACC receives these tokens and tries to match:

```text
switch_statement
        ↓
SWITCH LPAREN expression RPAREN
LBRACE case_list RBRACE
```

---

# 8. Understanding `case_list`

This rule:

```c
case_list:
        case_list case_block
        |
        case_list default_block
        |
        case_block
        |
        default_block
        ;
```

allows multiple cases.

For example:

```c
switch(x)
{
    case 1:
        break;

    case 2:
        break;

    case 3:
        break;

    default:
        break;
}
```

The parser can conceptually build:

```text
case_list
   │
   ├── case_block
   ├── case_block
   ├── case_block
   └── default_block
```

---

# 9. How `case_count` works in YACC

Look at:

```c
case_block:
        CASE constant COLON statement_list
        {
            case_count++;
            printf("Case label found\n");
        }
        ;
```

The part:

```c
CASE constant COLON statement_list
```

is the grammar.

The part:

```c
{
    case_count++;
    printf("Case label found\n");
}
```

is the **semantic action**.

So when YACC successfully recognizes an entire case block:

```text
CASE
  ↓
constant
  ↓
:
  ↓
statements
  ↓
SUCCESS
  ↓
case_count++
```

This is slightly different from your original Lex program.

---

# 10. Difference in counting

In your original Lex program:

```c
"case" {
    case_count++;
}
```

The counter increments as soon as Lex sees the word:

```text
case
```

In YACC:

```c
case_block:
    CASE constant COLON statement_list
    {
        case_count++;
    }
```

The counter increments **only after the complete case block has been successfully parsed**.

This is a better demonstration of syntax analysis.

---

# 11. `default` works similarly

You have:

```c
default_block:
        DEFAULT COLON statement_list
        {
            default_count++;
            printf("Default label found\n");
        }
        ;
```

The grammar is:

```text
default
   +
:
   +
statements
```

Once that entire structure is valid:

```text
default_count++
```

---

# 12. What does `expression` mean?

You have:

```c
expression:
        ID
        |
        INT_CONST
        |
        FLOAT_CONST
        |
        CHAR_CONST
        |
        expression PLUS expression
        |
        expression MINUS expression
        ...
```

This allows expressions such as:

```c
x
10
3.14
'a'
x + 10
x - 5
x * 2
x > 10
x == 5
x && y
```

So:

```c
switch(x)
```

works because:

```text
x
↓
ID
↓
expression
```

---

# 13. Why is `constant` separate?

You have:

```c
constant:
        INT_CONST
        |
        FLOAT_CONST
        |
        CHAR_CONST
        ;
```

This is useful because C `case` labels normally use a constant expression.

For example:

```c
case 1:
```

means:

```text
CASE
 ↓
INT_CONST
 ↓
COLON
```

And:

```c
case 'a':
```

means:

```text
CASE
 ↓
CHAR_CONST
 ↓
COLON
```

---

# 14. `statement_list`

You have:

```c
statement_list:
        statement_list statement
        |
        statement
        ;
```

This means:

> A statement list can contain one or more statements.

For example:

```c
case 1:
    printf("One");
    break;
```

has:

```text
statement_list
    │
    ├── printf(...)
    └── break
```

---

# 15. `statement`

You define different types of statements:

```c
statement:
        expression_statement
        |
        BREAK SEMICOLON
        |
        CONTINUE SEMICOLON
        |
        RETURN expression SEMICOLON
        |
        conditional_statement
        |
        loop_statement
        |
        function_call
        ;
```

So a `statement` could be:

```c
x = 10;
```

or:

```c
break;
```

or:

```c
continue;
```

or:

```c
return x;
```

or:

```c
if (...)
```

or:

```c
while (...)
```

or:

```c
printf(...);
```

---

# 16. What happens when syntax is wrong?

Suppose the user enters:

```c
switch(x) {
    case 1
        break;
}
```

Notice:

```text
case 1
```

is missing:

```text
:
```

The grammar expects:

```text
CASE constant COLON statement_list
```

but receives:

```text
CASE INT_CONST BREAK
```

YACC cannot match the grammar.

Therefore:

```c
yyerror(const char *s)
```

is called.

You will get something like:

```text
Syntax Error: syntax error
```

and:

```text
Parsing failed.
```

---

# 17. What happens with a valid input?

For:

```c
switch(x)
{
    case 1:
        printf("One");
        break;

    case 2:
        printf("Two");
        break;

    default:
        printf("Other");
}
```

The process is:

```text
switch
 ↓
SWITCH

(
 ↓
LPAREN

x
 ↓
ID

)
 ↓
RPAREN

{
 ↓
LBRACE

case
 ↓
CASE

1
 ↓
INT_CONST

:
 ↓
COLON
```

YACC matches:

```text
case_block
```

Then:

```text
case_count++
```

The second case does the same.

Then:

```text
default
```

matches:

```text
default_block
```

and:

```text
default_count++
```

Finally:

```text
}
```

matches `RBRACE`.

Then:

```text
switch_statement
```

is successfully recognized.

