# 18. `yyparse()` vs `yylex()`

This is another important viva question.

### In Lex:

```c
yylex();
```

means:

> Start lexical analysis.

### In YACC:

```c
yyparse();
```

means:

> Start syntax analysis.

But internally, YACC calls `yylex()` whenever it needs another token.

So:

```text
yyparse()
   │
   ├── asks yylex() for token
   │
   ├── receives token
   │
   ├── checks grammar
   │
   ├── asks yylex() for next token
   │
   ├── checks grammar
   │
   └── ...
```

This is extremely important.

---

# 19. Difference between your original program and YACC

| Your original FLEX program      | YACC version                           |
| ------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |
| Lexical analyzer                | Syntax analyzer                        |
| Identifies tokens               | Checks token arrangement               |
| Uses regular expressions        | Uses grammar/CFG rules                 |
| Uses `yylex()`                  | Uses `yyparse()`                       |
| `yytext` contains matched text  | Works mainly with tokens               |
| Counts `case` immediately       | Can count after complete grammar match |
| Finds operators                 | Checks expressions/statements          |
| Doesn't validate overall syntax | Validates syntax                       |
| `.l` file                       | `.y` file                              |

