## 21. One correction I'd recommend for your lab

The YACC grammar above is intentionally designed around the same features as your original program, but **C's actual expression grammar is more complicated** than the simplified `expression` rule shown here. Also, the `for` rule and function-call grammar are simplified.

If your lab question is specifically **"Implement a YACC program to recognize a switch-case statement"**, you actually don't need all the operators, loops, `printf`, `scanf`, etc. A much smaller grammar is easier to understand, easier to write in an exam, and much easier to explain during viva.

The core YACC grammar can be reduced to:

```text
switch_statement
        → SWITCH ( expression ) { case_list }

case_list
        → case_list case_block
        | case_block
        | case_list default_block
        | default_block

case_block
        → CASE constant : statement_list

default_block
        → DEFAULT : statement_list
```

That is the **actual YACC logic you should focus on understanding**.
